error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers most central arrangements. It names the board (implying a trust), the CCO under Rule 38a-1, the adviser, administrator/fund accounting, transfer agent, custodian, distributor, counsel, auditor and pricing vendors. It also lists APs, market makers/liquidity providers, exchange listing, DTC/NSCC clearing and creation/redemption processing (roughly the PCF workflow). It correctly treats the index licence and securities lending agent as conditional. It explicitly says roles may be bundled with one provider and that the adviser may house the CCO, so it does not imply every role needs a separate firm. However, it omits seed capital, a key ETF launch arrangement. It also has a localized error: it lists the Rule 22e-4 liquidity risk program as 'strategy-specific only (if applicable)'. Open-end funds, including ETFs, generally must adopt a liquidity risk management program; in-kind ETFs are relieved only of some elements. Listing a valuation designee as core is also slightly imprecise, since the board may perform fair value determinations itself. Explicit treatment of the fund/trust entity and board approval/oversight of the compliance program is thin.\"}","ttft_ms":4693,"type":"result","duration_ms":8540,"uuid":"3a031a33-e744-48ab-baa6-3ff3e25cfc4f","ttft_stream_ms":551,"time_to_request_ms":53,"first_content_frame_ms":551,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
